🐘 Snort Mastery: From Traffic Sniffing to Intrusion Prevention
Snort is a legendary open-source, lightweight Network Intrusion Detection System (NIDS) and Intrusion Prevention System (NIPS)
. Ranked as one of the most popular security tools globally, it allows analysts to manage network traffic based on specific, user-defined signatures known as Rules
.
⚙️ 1. Operational Architecture & Command Arsenal
Snort operates in several modes. As an analyst, you must master the command-line flags that control how Snort processes data and alerts your team.
Core Execution Flags
-c: Defines the path to the configuration file (e.g., snort.conf). This file tells Snort which rules and preprocessors to load
.
-T: Configuration Test. Always run this after modifying rules to ensure there are no syntax errors before deployment
.
-v / -d / -X: Control verbosity, from basic headers to full packet payload dumps
.
-r: Read Mode. Processes a saved .pcap file through the rule engine instead of live traffic [Conversation History].
-D: Daemon Mode. Runs Snort in the background for automated, permanent monitoring
.
Alerting Modes (-A)
The alert mode determines the detail level of your incident logs
:
console: Fast-style alerts displayed directly in your terminal—best for live testing
.
cmg: Displays the rule header plus the full payload in hex and text, essential for payload-level triage
.
fast: Summary info (IPs, timestamp, msg) written to a log file; no console output
.
full: (Default) Records every possible detail about the alert to the log file
.
none: Disables alerting entirely while still generating binary traffic logs
.
📝 2. The Anatomy of a Snort Rule
A rule is divided into two parts: the Header (mandatory) and the Options (enclosed in parentheses)
.
The Rule Header
Structure: [Action] [Protocol] [Src_IP] [Src_Port] [Direction] [Dst_IP] [Dst_Port]
Action: Common choices are alert (notify and log), log (silent log), drop (IPS mode: block and log), or reject (IPS mode: block and kill session)
.
Protocol: Snort 2 natively supports TCP, UDP, ICMP, and IP
.
IPs & Ports: Use any for universal matching or specific addresses/ranges (e.g., 192.168.1.0/24)
.
Negation (!): Use to exclude targets, such as !192.168.1.0/24 to monitor traffic outside your network
.
Direction: -> for one-way or <> for bidirectional traffic
.
🛠️ 3. Strategic Rule Examples (SOC Ready)
Example 1: Basic ICMP (Ping) Detection
alert icmp any any <> any any (msg: "ICMP Packet Found"; sid: 1000001; rev:1;)
Analyst Insight: This is the baseline rule used to confirm Snort is seeing traffic correctly
.
Example 2: Detecting a TCP SYN Scan (Reconnaissance)
alert tcp any any -> any any (msg: "TCP SYN Flag Detected"; flags:S; sid: 1000002; rev:1;)
Logic: Uses the flags option to look for the Synchronize bit, a precursor to port scanning activity
.
Example 3: Content Matching (HTTP GET Request)
alert tcp any any -> any 80 (msg: "GET Request Found"; content:"GET"; nocase; sid: 1000003; rev:1;)
Logic: Filters TCP traffic on port 80 and performs a case-insensitive search for "GET" in the payload
.
Example 4: Optimized Multi-Pattern Matching
alert tcp any any <> any 80 (msg: "HTTP GET for www"; content:"GET"; fast_pattern; content:"www"; sid:1000004; rev:1;)
Logic: The fast_pattern modifier speeds up performance by telling Snort which string to search for first
.
🛡️ 4. Transitioning to IPS Mode (Blocking Traffic)
Snort defaults to passive IDS mode. To turn it into an IPS that actively drops malicious packets, you must sit "inline" between network segments
.
Activation Command:
sudo snort -c /etc/snort/snort.conf -Q --daq afpacket -i eth0:eth1 -A console
Key Requirements:
Multiple Interfaces: IPS mode requires at least two interfaces (e.g., eth0:eth1) to pass traffic through the engine
.
Action Change: You must change your rule action from alert to drop or reject
.
💡 5. Senior Analyst "Pro-Tips"
SIDs (Security IDs): Always use a SID ≥ 1,000,000 for your custom rules to avoid overlapping with built-in rules (100–999,999)
.
Rule Location: Store your custom rules in /etc/snort/rules/local.rules
.
Evasion Awareness: Skilled attackers can bypass IDS rules using fragmentation (-f in Nmap) or timing controls
.
References: Always include a reference:cve,[ID]; in your rules. This helps junior analysts understand exactly which threat they are investigating
.
